# Process environment for the Rust Stado coordinator.
#
# Provider choice and backing-store locators intentionally do not live here.
# Install deploy/azure/stado.config.json at the path below and keep Azure
# primary, S3 backup, managed identity, release channel and agent grant in
# that single deployment config.
STADO_CONFIG=$HOME/.stado/config.json

# Coordinator/dashboard uses consumer stado-control-plane with owner-only grant
# ~/.stado/control-plane-skarbiec-token. Object routes fail closed unless it can
# read stado-object-api/token; machine submit/status/cancel independently require
# stado-machine-api/token; managed-service status/restart independently require
# stado-service-api/token. None has an environment or cross-route fallback.

# Azure VMs use the separate stado-azure-agent consumer. Its exact read:<item>
# allowlist equals agent.skarbiec.items; agent.skarbiec.secret_fields is the
# strictly smaller item#field set a job may project. stado-aws is available
# only to trusted agent storage code and is never a valid job secret reference.
# The opaque grant is delivered after VM creation in encrypted Azure Custom
# Script protectedSettings, which Azure omits from instance metadata and GET
# responses. The handler writes it to root-only tmpfs without argv/stdout; the
# Rust agent caches, overwrites and unlinks it on first use. customData contains
# only non-secret routing/allowlist values and startup blocks until handoff.

# Endpoint contract for consuming deployments. The public base is non-secret;
# trusted-service credentials stay only in the consuming server deployment.
# Provision each value through a non-rendering secret projection, never command
# output, logs, source, job JSON or VM customData:
#   STADO_API_URL=https://stado.wisent.com
#   STADO_API_TOKEN=<server-side projection of stado-object-api/token>
#   STADO_MACHINE_API_TOKEN=<server-side projection of stado-machine-api/token>
#   STADO_SERVICE_API_TOKEN=<server-side projection of stado-service-api/token>
# STADO_API_TOKEN authorizes object routes only. STADO_MACHINE_API_TOKEN
# authorizes exactly machine submit/status/cancel. STADO_SERVICE_API_TOKEN
# authorizes exactly managed-service status/restart. No token authorizes another
# family or falls through to Supabase. For Wisent App, mint consumer
# wisent-app-machine with only read:stado-machine-api and consumer
# wisent-app-service with only read:stado-service-api; never reuse the
# coordinator or agent grant for either projection.
#
# Public software release readers use the dedicated immutable release route:
#   https://stado.wisent.com/api/release/object?uri=<urlencoded-stado://releases/product/path>
# That route accepts GET only and refuses every namespace except `releases`.
# Release publishers still use authenticated STADO_API_URL + STADO_API_TOKEN
# object writes. User and product objects never use the release namespace and
# remain behind authenticated /api/object or a product-owned signed proxy.
# Never ship STADO_API_TOKEN, STADO_MACHINE_API_TOKEN or STADO_SERVICE_API_TOKEN to browsers.

# External prerequisites intentionally not provisioned by Stado:
# - Azure storage account and private stado container with the release tree.
# - User-assigned managed identity with Blob Data Contributor and VM
#   self-delete rights, attached through azure.vm_identity_id.
# - Per-region network resources and approved GPU quota.
# - S3 bucket plus stado-aws credentials.
# - Coordinator, restricted agent and consumer-specific Skarbiec grants described above.
# - TLS DNS/reverse proxy and a self-hosted control-plane runner.
